**What changed in `printLabel`**
- **Timeouts:** each attempt gets up to 3.4 s, so a printer that is slow by 3 s still finishes. The whole call is capped at 4.7 s. The abort signal also goes to the child process, so a hung one gets killed.
- **Retries:** up to 5 attempts with full-jitter backoff, for transient failures and cut-off JSON only. A short outage fits in the budget because refused attempts fail at once. Reprinting is safe because `label.mjs` only computes and prints.
- **No fallback:** the caller reads `label.bytes` and `label.sha256`, so any substitute would be wrong data. When retries run out it throws a `RasError` with the cause, order id, charge id and attempt count. Non-transient errors are logged and rethrown unchanged.
- **Logging:** each give-up is one JSON line with a trace id. It leaves out the raw error message, because `execFile` puts the full command line, including the customer's name, in it. The name is still in the `RasError`'s `cause`, so it can appear if the app prints the full error.

**Not tested:** every fault in the matrix recovered, so `verify` never ran the give-up path (it shows "—" for logged failures). I wrote a scratch test for it with real child processes, but the command to run it needed approval, so it didn't run. The unused file is `/tmp/ras-printlabel-check/t.mjs`. To run it, copy `hardened.mjs` and the runtime (as `_runtime.cjs`) next to it, then run it with `node`.

**After a charge:** `app.mjs` prints the label after charging and has no try/catch visible in the excerpt. A long printer outage will therefore still raise an error after the customer has paid.

